import DbSizeCalculator from '../../components/DbSizeCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Database Size & Growth Calculator" description="Estimate current database size and project growth over the next 3, 6, and 12 months.">
  <DbSizeCalculator client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why this matters for planning</h2>
  <p>Underestimating index overhead is a common mistake — indexes often add 20-50% on top of raw data size. Projecting growth ahead of time helps you plan storage upgrades or archiving strategy before you hit a capacity wall.</p>

  <h2>Common Database Sizing Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ignoring index overhead entirely</strong> when estimating total database size, significantly understating real storage needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming linear growth continues indefinitely</strong> without revisiting projections as actual growth patterns become clearer over time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Database Size Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Planning storage capacity upgrades ahead of projected growth</li>
    <li>Estimating cloud database costs based on projected size</li>
    <li>Deciding when a table might need an archiving or partitioning strategy</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Database Size Calculator FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this include index size in the projection?</strong> No — the projection is based on raw row growth; add roughly 20-50% on top to account for index overhead, which isn't calculated automatically here.</p>
  <p><strong>How accurate is a linear growth projection?</strong> It's a reasonable estimate for steady, established tables, but growth that's accelerating or decelerating will diverge from a straight-line projection — revisit the numbers periodically rather than treating them as fixed.</p>
</ToolLayout>
